ker threads. But there's something wrong with how the non-batch work is being constructed in the fallback case—the code seems to be filtering work items but it's cut off mid-condition.\n\nLet me trace through this more carefully. The thread buffer allocation for non-batch work uses `max_intermediates_len`, which starts at 1 and only gets increased for global AIRs that are large enough to skip batching. SCATTER AIRs don't affect this value, so if all the global AIRs end up in the batch, the buffer stays minimal—which is actually correct since the batch handles them.\n\nNow I'm wondering if the 416ms regression on LogUp GKR at APC 300 might stem from the intermediates offset overflowing. Let me check the math: with 300 AIRs, each potentially needing height × buffer_size entries, the worst case would be around 786 million entries or 12.6 GB. As a u32 count, that's still under the 4.2 billion limit, so overflow shouldn't be the culprit here.\n\nBut wait—12.6 GB vastly exceeds the 2 GB budget, so the budget check should catch this and fall back to the old path. Let me trace through the logic: if batch_intermediates_bytes exceeds 2 billion, use_batched becomes false and we should fall back, putting all work items into the non-batched path instead.\n\nThe problem is that max_intermediates_len was only computed from non_batch_indices, so when we fall back and push all items through the non-batched path, the per-thread buffers might be undersized for the batch-eligible items, potentially causing memory access violations or incorrect results. I need to figure out whether the issue is a buffer size mismatch in the fallback path or just slow performance in the batched kernel itself. Let me add some tracing to see what's actually happening with the budget check and intermediate buffer sizes for this specific case.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1836,"cache_read_input_tokens":95